<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  App Sandbox is deliberately OFF.

  This is not a shortcut. The sandbox forbids three things the app is built
  around, and each one is a core feature rather than a nicety:

    1. Spawning helper binaries. Piper, Tesseract and caffeinate are separate
       executables outside the bundle; a sandboxed process cannot exec them.
       Piper is the whole reason speech sounds like a person rather than a
       robot, and Tesseract is the only way a scanned book can be read at all.
    2. Scanning a library folder. The scanner walks a directory tree the user
       nominates once; the sandbox would restrict it to individually-picked
       files unless the whole scanner were rewritten around security-scoped
       bookmarks.
    3. Reaching Ollama on localhost, which is how AI features stay local and
       private rather than going to a cloud provider.

  The cost of this choice is that the app cannot ship on the Mac App Store. It
  ships as a notarized DMG from GitHub Releases instead, exactly as the Linux
  build ships as an AppImage — which is the distribution this project already
  targets.

  If Mac App Store distribution is ever wanted, re-enable app-sandbox here and
  budget for: bundling and co-signing Piper and Tesseract inside the bundle
  with the inherit entitlement, and rewriting the library scanner around
  security-scoped bookmarks. That is a project, not a flag.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Outbound HTTP: Ollama on localhost, cover art and dictionary lookups. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
